=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported inter-VLAN routing fault between VLAN 10 (PC4, 172.16.10.4) and VLAN 20
(Laptop0, 172.16.20.10) does not exist in the live lab. Every component in the forwarding
path was inspected and found to be correctly configured and operationally UP.

Router0 has both VLAN sub-interfaces active: eth0.10 carries 172.16.10.1/24 (the VLAN 10
gateway) and eth0.20 carries 172.16.20.1/24 (the VLAN 20 gateway). IPv4 forwarding is
enabled (ip_forward=1), and both subnets appear as directly connected routes in the kernel
table.

Switch1 correctly places PC4's port (eth1) into br_vlan10 and Laptop0's port (eth7) into
br_vlan20. The 802.1Q trunk toward mswitch1 (eth0.10 and eth0.20) is properly bridged into
the corresponding VLAN bridge domains.

Mswitch1's VLAN bridges are complete: br_vlan10 contains eth0.10 (sw1 trunk), eth2.10
(sw2 trunk), and eth4.10 (router0 trunk); br_vlan20 contains eth0.20, eth2.20, and
eth4.20 — the router0-facing tagged sub-interface for VLAN 20 is present and UP, which
was the suspected omission in the ticket.

A live ping from PC4 to Laptop0 returned 4/4 packets with 0% loss and sub-3 ms RTT.
Both hosts show their respective default gateways as ARP-REACHABLE.

Root cause: No fault exists. The ticket describes a condition that is not reproducible;
the inter-VLAN path is operating correctly end-to-end.

Corrective action: None required. Close the ticket as a false alarm; advise the user to
retest from their endpoint, as the issue may have been a transient host-side ARP or
temporary convergence event that has since cleared.
=== END ===
